    "turn_text": "Yeah, I mean, it's a few things. Like I'd say, first off, there's a lot of people who came before me who made it possible for me to be here and not just be here to exist in these halls, but be here. And I think we've had a really successful freshman term thus far and been able to get through some big things. The first bill I introduced was created federal office of gun violence prevention. It's hard to create a federal office like that in your first term in the minority. And the president took my piece of legislation and implemented it in the executive order. And now we have it. And I was with him at the White House when he put that into action. So these things don't just happen. And it's not just because of me, obviously our office played a huge role in it, but it's because of organizers, advocates and the folks who came before me who made it possible for a very young freshman member of the minority to be able to still get things done. And like, you know, I'm part of leadership for certain caucuses among the House Oversight Committee, which is like probably one of the most publicly public facing committees. And so you think about people like, you know, young people who came before, people like AOC, right, who like helped pave the way for young people. Or you think about the fact that I'm Afro Latino, someone like Richie Torres, you know, when he came in, there was a big kind of public drama around him being a part of the Black Caucus and Hispanic Caucus, because he went through that I didn't have to. And you know what, I just signed up for both that I was in both that I didn't go through a big drama. And it just shows that like the people who've come before us are really important in our journeys. And sometimes we think about those people, we think, oh, it's just the old folks or just our ancestors, which they are, but it's also going to be people who work close to you in age who are just like right there with you, who have done something that kind of opened that pathway.",
