    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, I'm part of the minority of people who actually, I think polls are actually very useful if you understand what they are, right? Like it's not. It's not a magic ball that says on November 4th or 6th, whatever election day is this year, this will happen. But what it does tell you is just a snapshot in time what would happen if the election were held now. What might happen if the election were held now? And I think it's really important to know because once you fully understand what a poll is and you don't over-blow it or under-sell it, it gives you the ability to do better in your campaign. You know, yes, I've seen some very concerning polls as far as the president's reelection efforts concerned. I'm part of the president's national advisory committee for his campaign and had and will be doing a lot more traveling to help make sure that we, you know, keep neo-fascist, right with a Christian nationalist Donald Trump out of office this year. And the polls tell us a few things. They've been trending in a better direction. which is good for us. Those are very concerning there for a minute and still concerning, but we're getting in a better place. As far as the youth vote is concerned, obviously, it keeps fluctuating in big ways. But what I've seen is that young people are concerned about a lot of different issues going on, both domestically and internationally. And I think it's important that we speak to those issues, but that we meet people where they're at. And I think oftentimes there's There's a lot going on right now. And, you know, sometimes there's a hesitancy to engage with young people because you're afraid of what they'll say or what they'll do. And what I've always encouraged my colleagues to do and the campaign to is like walk into the fire, you know, walk into places where you might be a little uncomfortable and connect with folks. Number one, you're going to have a lot of conversations that might surprise you that will surprise you because young people are not a modelist. Not everyone agrees on the same thing. But number two, you gain respect from people when you have the ability to, like, come into space and debate with them or talk with them about an issue that you care about, and it shows that you care about them. It's not just about inviting young people to your table, but it's about you going to theirs, whether that's a college campus, community college, concerts, wherever people are at, and I think that's really important. So we're getting close to election day. I'm not going to say we have so much time, but we do have time here to, of course, correct and get to a better place. And the administration is listening to young people, which I think is really helpful. And we got to keep in mind what's at stake here. I like to get really personal with people from an organizing perspective to explain this. We talked about my first bill, federal office of gun violence prevention. Such a dope office. It's not just like an office, right? I wouldn't. I'm not the type to go to the White House just for like a photo op, right? Like this office is real. And the first thing I asked when they told me about it was awesome. Who's going to run it? They have survivors of gun violence who have been doing this work. Someone like Greg Jackson, who's a good friend of mine. He was the former executive director of community justice action fund, the only really national gun violence prevention organization that's black led and centers the experiences of black and brown people. And he's the deputy director of that office. That office helped me get $1.5 million for the city of Orlando's community violence intervention program. What's community violence intervention? Well, glad you asked. What it is, is they're programs that put money into the hands of the community. So that way community members can identify who's more likely to be shot and who's more likely to shoot someone. And they literally intervene, creating relationships with shooters, with gang members, with people who a lot of times are part of a lot of the violence in our communities and they work at breaking the cycle of violence. In my city of Orlando, since this program has started, we've seen gun violence go down. And so we want to continue to fund those programs. What I was on the streets of Orlando, you know, for the Black Lives Matter protests where I was tear gas, maced, arrested, I was jailed in my district for non-violent protests. Part of what I was fighting for was community involvement and control and public safety, right? That's really what we're fighting for, investing in our communities. President Biden's done that with the Office of Violence Prevention. If President Trump were elected, guess what he would do on day one. get rid of the office, it would just not exist anymore. That money would go away, the resources would go away, and I take that really personally. That alone is enough for me personally to like go all in on this election, but there's like a million, no, not say a million there, but there are many, many, many examples of that same thing happening that can really impact all of our communities. So I say that, you know, another type of person to shame people for not wanting to vote, I get it. Our system sucks. And there's a lot wrong with it. But our adversaries in this moment and our opponents want us to walk away because they're more than happy to step in. And that's",
