    "turn_idx": 197,
    "turn_text": "Well, I'll tell you, it's like, look, if you want to work, if you want to work the third party angle, do it. Just know, though, that there are huge implications and vulnerabilities and risks that comes with doing that at the residential level, and it's also not how third parties are created. If you just look in the history of how parties are created in other countries and Latin American countries, if you don't feel represented by either party, build a third party, but you must start at the local level. That's how you build some sort of third party. Do not try to do it at the presidential level because not only are you going to not be successful in the current system we have, but you could really have an impact that could mess us up and like elect a Donald Trump or something like that. And I think it's important to know that. I think a lot of times people, there's a lot of people would say they're acceleration us where they just want to everything like the next moment and they'll do it by any means necessary. That's not part of my theory of change and I think it harms almost vulnerable communities. And so thinking about that, it's like, yeah, you can vote for President Biden because you hate Donald Trump. Maybe you're not super excited about President Biden and still, you know, run a candidate or work as a candidate for city council in a third party",
